  "data": {
    "A": "Research the sparse-obstacle route: deduplicate and sort blocked coordinates, handle blocked start or destination, and use factorial-based first-hit/inclusion-exclusion counting when the unique obstacle count is sufficiently low. Compare crossover rules using workload characteristics rather than test-specific values; retain a linear-memory rolling-row fallback for denser cases. Check duplicates, coordinate ordering, n=1, and modular arithmetic against the oracle.",
    "B": "Research the dense rolling-row kernel on the ARM64 target. The measured four-lane and eight-cell NEON variants were 0.033389s and 0.032195s respectively, so verify that the modest apparent gain is repeatable and useful; retain a portable scalar fallback. Preserve exact segmented-prefix behavior at blocked cells and validate tails, carry reductions, and duplicate obstacles.",
    "C": "Research the anti-diagonal tiled DP, the fastest reported candidate at 0.023443s. Explore tile size, work-sharing threshold, and synchronization cost for the four-core target; in particular, test whether parity-indexed boundary buffers can reduce synchronization without breaking wave dependencies. Keep a low-overhead fallback for smaller grids and validate boundary tiles and obstacle handling.",
    "G": "Once tree candidates are available, compare them as complete programs, not as additive speedups. Treat the 0.023443s tiled and 0.024859s sparse/dense candidates as promising measured variants, subject to correctness validation. Evaluate a general workload-based combination—sparse counting for sufficiently few unique obstacles, tiled DP for suitable large grids, and a rolling-row fallback—against each standalone candidate on the unchanged target and harness. Keep only oracle-correct variants, including for blocked endpoints, duplicates, n=1, and modular results; do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source files in this synthesis phase.",
    "analysis": "The supplied source is Problem D: count monotone grid paths modulo 1,000,000,007. Its correct baseline takes 0.081708s and allocates an N×N blocked-cell structure despite using rolling DP. The target is ARM64 with four cores. Reported candidate means are 0.023443s for C's tiled wavefront, 0.024859s for A's sparse/dense approach, 0.032195s for B's eight-cell NEON scan, 0.033389s for B's four-lane scan, 0.044173s for another C hybrid, and 0.051226s for another A variant. These measurements motivate complementary sparse, dense-kernel, and parallel-wavefront research; they do not establish correctness or predict integrated performance."
